      question: "Describe your coaching approach.",
      competency: "Coaching method, diagnosis, ownership",
      script: "My coaching approach is to understand before I advise. I usually start with questions: what are you trying to accomplish, where are you stuck, what have you already tried, and what kind of support would be useful? That helps me avoid solving the wrong problem.\n\nOnce I understand the blocker, I adjust the support. Sometimes the person needs confidence, sometimes a clearer standard, sometimes a specific example, and sometimes a better way to think through the decision. My goal is not to make the work sound like me. It is to help the person make a better decision and still own the solution.\n\nA recent example is from The Learning Studio, the monthly learning sessions I lead for more than 100 learning professionals. One team member volunteered to teach designers how to edit HTML code for Articulate Rise using AI. When she first showed me the deck, I asked what she wanted designers to be able to do after the session and which parts she thought they would find hardest.\n\nHer answer helped clarify the real issue. The content was accurate, but the session was starting too deep in HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. The audience did not need a programming lesson first. They needed to see what useful output they could create. I suggested opening with a practical before-and-after: show an unattractive interaction, use a good prompt, then reveal the improved activity. She kept ownership of the session, revised the deck, and the feedback after the session was very positive.",
