      question: "Tell me about a time you coached someone and helped them grow.",
      competency: "Coaching, feedback, capability building",
      script: "One strong example is from the 3E onboarding project at Thomson Reuters. I was mentoring a newer instructional designer while we were building practice cases for a complex technical product. This was not a simple conversion project, and she was still building proficiency in Storyline.\n\nI did not want to simply mark up her slides and tell her what to fix. When we reviewed her work, I would ask what she wanted the learner to notice, what job aid the question connected to, and where the learner might get stuck. That helped us separate two issues: the content logic and the design execution.\n\nOn content, I coached her to refine the practice questions and feedback so they gave enough guidance without giving away the answer. On design, I pushed for consistency and precision, but I also explained why it mattered. The point was not decoration or pixel perfection for its own sake. The design had to make the troubleshooting path easier to follow.\n\nThat project also gave her room to practice more advanced Storyline work, including variables and triggers. Later, on the 3E Cloud project, she needed less guidance. I helped her prepare questions for SME discovery calls and gave feedback on diagrams, but she was able to own more of the work. That is the coaching result I want: not just a better deliverable, but a stronger designer.",
