      type: "question",
      number: 12,
      group: "performance",
      question: "How do you diagnose development needs?",
      competency: "Needs analysis, performance consulting, conceptual analysis",
      script: "I try to diagnose the work before I diagnose the person. I start with the business goal, then ask what people need to do differently to reach that goal. After that, I look for the gap: is it knowledge, skill, confidence, motivation, environment, process, tools, manager reinforcement, or unclear expectations?\n\nI adjust the approach based on the size of the problem. For a large-scope program like Engage and Connect, I used a more ADDIE-style approach: examine performance metrics, understand the communication behaviors driving the gap, design the curriculum around those behaviors, then evaluate the result. For a smaller targeted issue like ACS at Thomson Reuters, I used a more iterative approach. Leaders asked us to make the training more engaging, but the report showed the real gap was probing. So I built a scenario-based ILT in PowerPoint, tested the decision paths through discussion and feedback, and the next cohort improved in probing.\n\nSo my approach is to separate the request from the real need. If it is a skill problem, training or practice may help. If it is a process, tool, reinforcement, or expectation problem, the solution has to match that gap.",
